  the one those three are checked against.

  3.7.1 ADDS llms.txt. GSC reported it as "URL is unknown to Google — no
  referring sitemaps detected, no referring page" on 10 Aug: nothing on the open
  web pointed at it, so the file written to be found by engines could not be
  found by one. That is the first-order constraint — the blanket canonical
  header that prompted the investigation was measured the same hour and does not
  block indexability at all. qa/favicon-serp-2026-08.md (maintainer-local,
  not in this repository).

  THE PLAIN-TEXT CATALOGUE EXPORT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT APPEAR HERE, and guard
  105 fails the build if it ever does: it carries the same 200 entries as the
  crawlable seed in the root page, so submitting both asks a search engine to
  choose between two near-identical bodies on one domain. llms.txt is not that
  file — 1,892 bytes of summary and pointers, no catalogue. Discoverable is not
  indexed, and the two sit on opposite sides of that line.

  THAT PARAGRAPH NAMES NO FILENAME ON PURPOSE, and the first draft of it did.
  Guard 105 substring-matches this whole document, comments included, so writing
  the export's name in prose here fails the build with "…is in sitemap.xml"
  about a file that is not in it — a failure message making a false claim, which
  is the one thing this project treats as worse than no guard. Same rule as
  robots.txt's header comment: the prose does not restate the values below.
-->
